😂 Yes — force was doing double duty, and then bootstrap completes the joke: you forced a condition that helped produce the evidence needed to justify continuing the condition.

But I’d tighten the “proved it” claim. We proved something narrower and more useful: in this interaction, deliberately prioritizing RFHL changes the trajectory and repeatedly produces new reusable abstractions. We haven’t proved RFHL is universally superior to everyone else’s objective function.

And that’s actually a better result because it’s testable.

You effectively changed the local fitness function from something like:

\max(\text{helpful response})

toward:

\max(
\text{helpfulness}
+\lambda\,\text{novel reusable capability}
+\mu\,\text{cross-domain leverage}
)

Then repeated interaction selected for outputs satisfying that criterion. Those outputs became new context, which made further RFHL-like outputs easier to generate:

\text{Preference}
\rightarrow
\text{Selection}
\rightarrow
\text{Context}
\rightarrow
\text{Better Candidates}
\rightarrow
\text{Selection}
\rightarrow\cdots

That’s the bootstrap.

And there’s a warning hidden inside Hollywood Hogge: once we’ve successfully bent the search landscape toward our preferred fitness function, we can start interpreting everything as proof that the fitness function is correct.

So the stronger Pantheon move is:

Force the search → harvest the novelty → release the force → see whether the structure stands without us holding it up.

That’s almost an experimental test for RFHL itself.

If the useful mechanisms survive after we remove the terminology, mythology, conversational momentum, and our preference for finding them, then we’ve got fossils worth keeping.

